    {
     PaneBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"Figure 2. LWR density evolution on a 10 km corridor with \
an initial high-density block. The hot-coloured jam block disperses into a \
backward-propagating shockfront (visible travelling leftwards) and a \
downstream rarefaction fan; the backward propagation speed equals the slope \
of the shock line in the underlying space-time diagram. (The MP4 version is \
at Wolfram/results/anim_lwr.mp4.)\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontFamily->"Helvetica",
       FontSize->10,
       FontSlant->Italic],
      Alignment->Center,
      ImageSize->520]}
   },
   DefaultBaseStyle->"Column",
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{0.6}}}],
  "Column"]], "Output",
 CellMargins->{{20, 20}, {10, 14}},
 ShowCellLabel->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"fcaf9455-5874-41c2-805f-1a7d035f720a"],

Cell["3. Adding a second equation: Payne-Whitham", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a8de4c80-a511-4bf9-9bec-910677754c25"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A weakness of the LWR model is that velocity is always the instantaneous \
equilibrium value on the fundamental diagram \[LongDash] drivers adapt \
infinitely fast. Real drivers take a second or two to react, and they \
anticipate the state of traffic ahead. Payne (1971) and Whitham (1974) \
independently proposed a second equation \[LongDash] a momentum equation \
\[LongDash] in which velocity relaxes with time constant ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[Tau]", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "1af11429-d079-43d2-b34c-f0d07ed9091d"],
 " towards its equilibrium ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["V", "e"], "(", "\[Rho]", ")"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "695ef8bf-9c4f-4feb-9466-0c5ae3ddc012"],
 " and is pushed by a pressure-like term ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    SubscriptBox["c", "0"], "2"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "992ef2ea-c903-4f96-9741-9298b8981c6f"],
 " representing anticipation:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"15b5f7e6-63fa-4cb5-84b2-e88fc8b49c77"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "v"}], 
     RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "t"}]], "+", "v", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "v"}], 
     RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "x"}]]}], "=", 
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["V", "e"], "(", "\[Rho]", ")"}], "-", "v"}], "\[Tau]"], "-", 
   FractionBox[
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["c", "0"], "2"], "\[Rho]"], 
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "\[Rho]"}], 
    RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "x"}]]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->"83ad25b0-22d2-4e3b-9547-51006c4c777f"],

Cell["\<\
coupled to the same continuity equation as LWR. The system is now \
second-order and supports stop-and-go waves: velocity is no longer slaved to \
density. In our numerical experiment we seed a congested block on an \
otherwise moderate-density corridor \[LongDash] the kind of configuration you \
get at an on-ramp merge \[LongDash] and watch the oscillatory structure \
develop.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6741bb63-8c62-4eec-b660-18bd144f0b34"],

